00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:51.059
so they kind of you know engage in

00:25:51.059 --> 00:25:53.159
behaviors that in theory are like

00:25:53.159 --> 00:25:55.799
maximized by uh the company&#x27;s bottom

00:25:55.799 --> 00:25:57.659
line at the end of the day

00:25:57.659 --> 00:25:58.320
um

00:25:58.320 --> 00:26:00.360
I think to like fully understand what&#x27;s

00:26:00.360 --> 00:26:02.760
going on here it makes sense to take a

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:05.340
second and like really empathize with a

00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:08.400
grant Giver so if you&#x27;re Susan G Komen

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:09.299
right

00:26:09.299 --> 00:26:12.600
you have a million dollars to give to uh

00:26:12.600 --> 00:26:14.460
breast cancer research and there are

00:26:14.460 --> 00:26:15.960
five different research groups that

00:26:15.960 --> 00:26:18.360
apply to receive this million dollars of

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:20.100
funding like what are you going to do

00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:22.380
how do you decide like who to give the

00:26:22.380 --> 00:26:25.080
money to that in theory has the best

00:26:25.080 --> 00:26:27.419
chance of giving you a good Roi on that

00:26:27.419 --> 00:26:29.400
funding and then eventually like

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:31.080
improving the fight against breast

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:32.220
cancer

00:26:32.220 --> 00:26:33.360
um

00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:36.539
so the issue is that it&#x27;s basically

00:26:36.539 --> 00:26:38.279
impossible to holistically measure

00:26:38.279 --> 00:26:40.440
scientific quality there are plenty of

00:26:40.440 --> 00:26:43.260
examples where a very very important

00:26:43.260 --> 00:26:45.779
findings were not recognized until after

00:26:45.779 --> 00:26:47.640
the researcher&#x27;s career kind of like

00:26:47.640 --> 00:26:50.220
artists to a certain degree and so we&#x27;re

00:26:50.220 --> 00:26:52.679
kind of stuck with these imperfect like

00:26:52.679 --> 00:26:55.620
metrics uh where we sort of like infer

00:26:55.620 --> 00:26:57.600
scientific quality or at least funders

00:26:57.600 --> 00:26:59.640
use these metrics to infer scientific

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:01.740
quality and inform their own Grant

00:27:01.740 --> 00:27:03.299
making decisions

00:27:03.299 --> 00:27:05.640
um and so these are essentially called

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:08.520
bibliometrics uh bibliometrics or

00:27:08.520 --> 00:27:11.460
citation based metrics are used to

00:27:11.460 --> 00:27:14.159
quantify researcher&#x27;s productivity so

00:27:14.159 --> 00:27:16.380
how often they publish and then their

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:19.260
impact so when they do publish how often

00:27:19.260 --> 00:27:20.820
do other researchers basically

00:27:20.820 --> 00:27:23.880
incorporate their science into their own

00:27:23.880 --> 00:27:25.080
work

00:27:25.080 --> 00:27:27.600
um in a way that it&#x27;s cited within the

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:31.200
second scientists eventual publication

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:33.179
um the way that we actually measure this

00:27:33.179 --> 00:27:35.159
in today&#x27;s world is something called an

00:27:35.159 --> 00:27:36.840
impact factor this is kind of like

00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:39.120
getting out of Vogue a little bit now

00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:41.100
but it&#x27;s a quality metric for a journal

00:27:41.100 --> 00:27:43.860
so it&#x27;s the number of citations divided

00:27:43.860 --> 00:27:46.080
by the total number of papers uh within

00:27:46.080 --> 00:27:47.520
the journal and it&#x27;s normally averaged

00:27:47.520 --> 00:27:49.679
out over a two-year period

00:27:49.679 --> 00:27:51.900
um we also have an H index um which is

00:27:51.900 --> 00:27:54.299
like kind of complicated to explain but

00:27:54.299 --> 00:27:56.220
it&#x27;s more or less like a average number

00:27:56.220 --> 00:27:58.500
of citations per paper that you put out

00:27:58.500 --> 00:28:00.720
with some of the outliers kind of like

00:28:00.720 --> 00:28:02.279
softened away

00:28:02.279 --> 00:28:03.659
and so

